# L §2.5 Keys at a stepped range (tally 2.1.7 B1, issue #150; #134a, F19, P65): a key is one of the range's
# values a + i·s <= b by its value, so the int 2 and the number 2.0 are the key 2 of 0.5 .. 2.5 step 0.5, a pin
# written by_frac[2.0] pins it, and 3 is refused with RUN-KEY's form. 2.1.6's refcore refused LOAD-KEY 2 (the
# range enumerated into a set of its values' forms) and LOAD-LITERAL for the pin's 2.0. (T's G13 pz_p2, r_p_q1.)
=== frac.tally
model frac
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "made up"

input by_frac[f in 0.5 .. 2.5 step 0.5]: num

input two_i: int

input two_f: num

ask q_int: by_frac[two_i]

ask q_num: by_frac[two_f]

ask q_pin: by_frac[two_i] as if {by_frac[2.0] = 8}

ask q_off: by_frac[two_i + 1]

ask q_sum: sum(by_frac[f] for f in 0.5 .. 2.5 step 0.5)

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  by_frac[*] = 1 "the card"
  by_frac[2] = 5 "the card"
  two_i = 2 "the card"
  two_f = 2.0 "the card"
=== expect
q_int: 5
  cut 2026-03-01
q_num: 5
  cut 2026-03-01
q_off: refused: RUN-KEY 3 is not a value of 0.5 .. 2.5 step 0.5
  cut 2026-03-01
q_pin: 8
  pinned: by_frac[2] = 8
  cut 2026-03-01
q_sum: 9
  cut 2026-03-01
